---
title: 'Shadow DOM'
description: "$framework Charts can render a chart in a shadow root of its own, so the page's CSS cannot restyle it."
---

Rendering a chart in a shadow root stops the page's CSS from interfering with it. Use this when page styles change how the chart's controls look, such as its legend items.

## Isolating a Chart from Page Styles

{% chartExampleRunner title="Page Styles" name="page-styles" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    enableShadowDom: true,
}
```

In this example:

- The page has a `button:not(#toolbar button)` rule. The ID gives it enough weight to outrank the chart's styles, so it restyles the legend items.
- Select Shadow Root to set `enableShadowDom: true`, and the legend items go back to the chart's own styling.

Page CSS that refers to the chart's elements, such as the `.ag-charts-tooltip` rules in [Tooltips](./tooltips/), does not reach a chart in a shadow root. [Theme](./themes/) options and CSS variables still apply.

## Automatic Detection

When `enableShadowDom` is not set, the chart uses a shadow root only on known pages whose CSS breaks its controls, such as some AI chat visualisations. Set `enableShadowDom: false` to keep the chart in the page's DOM on those pages.

